---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import TableScroll from "../components/TableScroll.astro";
import Toc from "../components/Toc.astro";
import { stores } from "../data/stores";
import runningShot from "../assets/screenshots/running.png";

const sections = {
  steps: "What happens on each store",
  permissions: "The permissions it asks for",
  hosts: "The sites it connects to",
  storage: "What it stores, and for how long",
  "delivery-location": "What it changes on other Amazon stores",
  "remote-code": "No remote code",
} as const;

const tocItems = Object.entries(sections).map(([id, label]) => ({ href: `#${id}`, label }));

const amazonHosts = stores.map((store) => `www.${store.domain}`);
---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="What Across Amazon does during a check, the permissions and sites it needs, what it stores and what it changes on other Amazon stores."
>
  <PageIntro
    title="How it works"
    lede="Across Amazon compares the delivered price of the product you are viewing with the same product on other Amazon stores. This page explains each step, what the extension needs and what it leaves behind."
  />
  <div class="container page-body">
    <Toc items={tocItems} />

    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        Opening the popup only reads the product page you are on. Nothing else happens until you press the check
        button, which reads <span class="nowrap">“Check 9 other Amazons”</span> with the default settings. The
        extension then looks for the same product on each other store, three stores at a time, reading their pages in
        the background. A check of nine stores takes about 15 seconds. It reads what each store charges to deliver the
        product to you and ranks the stores by that delivered total, converted to one currency at the European Central
        Bank’s daily rate.
      </p>

      <h2 id="steps">{sections.steps}</h2>
      <p>Three stores are checked at a time. For each store, the extension does this:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Looks up your product by its ASIN.</strong> It downloads the store’s page for your product’s ASIN,
          Amazon’s own product identifier. If that page shows your product and it can be bought, that is an exact
          match. When the store does not list the ASIN, or its listing is unavailable, it searches.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sets the delivery location, if needed.</strong> On the first page it reads on the store, it reads
          which country the store delivers to. If the store delivers elsewhere, it sets the store’s delivery location to
          your destination country and reads the page again, so the price it reads is quoted for your destination. The
          store is left that way afterwards: see <a href="#delivery-location">what it changes on other Amazon stores</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Searches, if the store does not list the ASIN or its listing is unavailable.</strong> It searches the
          store, first for the brand and model number when the title has one, then for the title, brand and selected
          variant. Each result is scored on how closely its title, brand and variant match your product. The best
          result must pass the matching threshold. Its page is then checked for your colour, pack and size. When it
          shows another variant, the extension reads the page of the variant it offers with your attributes instead;
          when the page does not offer it, the store is reported as{" "}
          <span class="nowrap">“No confident match”</span>. A store where no result passes the threshold is reported
          the same way.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reads the prices.</strong> It reads the item price, the shipping and any import fees the page quotes
          for your destination. Their sum is the delivered total, in the currency the page shows. When a store shows
          no import fees for your destination, the total is item plus shipping and
          the popup <a href="/faq/#import-fees">says so</a>, because duties may still be due on delivery. If Amazon
          shows a higher total than the sum, that total is used. When the page shows no price for your destination but
          offers <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span>, and does not say the item is currently
          unavailable, it loads that list of other sellers’ new offers, up to three pages of it, as the button would,
          and quotes the offer that is cheapest delivered at the day’s rate among the new offers whose item and
          delivery prices (or free delivery) it could read and convert. The result names the seller and the dispatcher
          as far as Amazon’s offer shows them, says how many offers the quote was chosen from, and shows no import fees.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Opens a tab only when it must.</strong> From version 0.2.0 the pages are downloaded with your Amazon
          session and read in a hidden page of the extension, which runs none of Amazon’s scripts and loads none of its
          images or ads. A page that cannot be read that way opens in a background tab that never takes focus, for
          example a robot check, a download that fails or takes longer than 15 seconds, a page the extension does not
          recognise or that leads to another Amazon store, a page that shows another delivery destination than the one
          the store is set to, a location change the store refuses from the extension, or an offers list it cannot
          load. The tab closes as soon as the page has been read. Versions before 0.2.0 open every page in a background
          tab, one store at a time.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        While the check runs, the popup shows the results so far. The dial counts the stores finished out of the
        total. A line names the step of the store that moved on
        last: <em>Checking</em>, <em>Searching</em>, <em>Matching product</em> or <em>Reading cost stack</em>, with
        “+2” when two more stores are being checked. Each finished store gets a chip with its delivered total in your
        display currency, or a dash when it could not be compared, and a blue “…” chip marks each store still being
        checked.
      </p>
      <Shot
        src={runningShot}
        alt="Running popup with a progress dial at 3 of 9, Searching France +2, chips for Canada, Mexico and the United Kingdom, and Germany, France and Italy in progress"
        width={360}
      >
        <strong>A check in progress.</strong> Three of nine stores are done; Germany, France and Italy are being
        checked, France being searched. Prices are illustrative.
      </Shot>
      <p>
        <span class="nowrap">“Stop checking”</span> cancels the run, stops the downloads in progress and closes any
        background tab it opened. A check stops after 4 minutes, and a store not finished by then is listed as
        <span class="nowrap">“Could not read the page”</span>. Closing the popup does not stop a check: it finishes in
        the background and its result is stored, unless Amazon showed a robot check or the check ran out of time.
        Reopen the popup on the same tab to follow its progress.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the last store is done, the popup ranks every store that quoted a delivered total, cheapest first,
        including the page you are on. The card at the top names the cheapest. Stores that could not be compared are
        listed under “N marketplaces could not be compared”, each with the reason. Press{" "}
        <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> within 30 minutes and the same result comes back at once from
        storage, with nothing downloaded again and no tabs opened. A check in which Amazon showed a robot check, or
        that ran out of time, is not stored, so <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> checks every store again.
      </p>
      <p>
        A page opened in a background tab is an ordinary visit and appears in Chrome’s history; a page downloaded in
        the background (version 0.2.0 and later) does not. If you are signed in to Amazon, the pages it reads may
        appear in your Amazon browsing and search history. They may also influence Amazon’s recommendations.
      </p>

      <h2 id="permissions">{sections.permissions}</h2>
      <p>
        Apart from the sites in the <a href="#hosts">next section</a>, the extension asks Chrome for these permissions.
        Versions before 0.2.0 ask only for <code>scripting</code> and <code>storage</code>.
      </p>
      <TableScroll label="Permissions">
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Permission</th>
              <th scope="col">Why it is needed</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>offscreen</code></th>
              <td>
                From version 0.2.0. During a check the extension opens one hidden page of its own, an offscreen
                document. It downloads the other stores’ product and search pages with your Amazon session and reads
                each one as data, in a document that runs none of the page’s scripts and loads none of its images or
                ads, with the extension’s own packaged scripts. Chrome shows no warning for this permission. The
                document is closed after the check.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>scripting</code></th>
              <td>
                The extension declares no content scripts, so none of its code runs on a page by itself. When you open
                the popup on one of the ten stores, and when you start a check, it injects its own packaged scripts
                into the Amazon tab you are on and into any background tab it opens on the other stores: from version
                0.2.0 only for a page that cannot be read in the offscreen document, before that for every page. The
                scripts read the product, its price, shipping and import fees and the delivery destination, and set a store’s
                delivery country when needed. As each background tab loads, they check that it shows the store’s page
                and not Amazon’s robot check. On a store page that is not a product page, the extension finds no
                product and keeps nothing.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>storage</code></th>
              <td>
                Keeps your settings (which stores to check and the display currency), the result of a finished check so
                that checking again within 30 minutes returns it at once, and the day’s exchange rates so they are
                normally downloaded once per UTC day. All of it stays in your browser.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableScroll>

      <h2 id="hosts">{sections.hosts}</h2>
      <p>
        The extension’s manifest lists twelve hosts. Its own requests go to them and to Amazon’s image servers, for the
        product picture shown in the popup. A page it downloads loads nothing else; a page it opens in a background
        tab loads whatever Amazon’s own pages load, such as Amazon’s image and script servers. Nothing goes to a server
        of the developer: the extension has none.
      </p>
      <TableScroll label="Hosts in the extension’s manifest">
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Host</th>
              <th scope="col">Why it connects</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                The ten Amazon stores
                <ul class="host-list" role="list">
                  {
                    amazonHosts.map((host) => (
                      <li>
                        <code>{host}</code>
                      </li>
                    ))
                  }
                </ul>
              </th>
              <td>
                From version 0.2.0 the offscreen document downloads the other stores’ product and search pages and
                sends their own <span class="nowrap">“Choose your location”</span> requests and their requests for
                other sellers’ offers with your Amazon session. A page that cannot be read that way, and every page
                before 0.2.0, opens in a background tab, where the extension’s scripts read it and send those requests
                from the store’s own page. The extension also runs its scripts in the Amazon tab you are on, and the
                popup uses this access to see that the tab you are on is an Amazon store, without the broader{" "}
                <code>tabs</code> permission. The other sellers’ offers are asked for during a check, on a product page
                that shows no price but offers <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span>, as Amazon’s button
                does.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>www.ecb.europa.eu</code></th>
              <td>
                The extension downloads the European Central Bank’s daily euro reference rates, which convert each
                store’s delivered total into one currency. This normally happens once per UTC day. Only a successful
                download is kept, so after a failure it is tried again on the next check.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>api.frankfurter.dev</code></th>
              <td>
                A fallback for the same daily rates, used only when the European Central Bank’s feed cannot be loaded.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableScroll>
      <p>
        Your browser makes the Amazon requests with your own Amazon session. The stores receive only what a
        comparison needs: your destination country in their location request, the product’s
        ASIN in the addresses of the product pages and, with the page’s own parameters, a new-offers filter and a page
        number, in the request for other sellers’ offers, and its title, brand, model number and selected variant as
        search terms. If you are signed in, Amazon can link these requests to your account. From version 0.2.0, the
        requests the extension makes in the background come from the extension rather than from a store’s page, so
        Amazon can tell them apart from your own visits, and the <span class="nowrap">“Choose your location”</span>
        change it sends names the extension in its Origin header. The exchange rate requests carry no information
        about you or the product. Like any web request, they let those servers see your IP address.
      </p>

      <h2 id="storage">{sections.storage}</h2>
      <p>
        Everything is kept in the extension’s local storage in your browser: your settings (which stores to check and
        the display currency), the result of each finished check and each day’s exchange rates.
      </p>
      <TableScroll label="Stored data">
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What</th>
              <th scope="col">How long</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your settings</th>
              <td>Until you change them or remove the extension.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Check results</th>
              <td>
                A finished check is reused for 30 minutes, unless Amazon showed a robot check during it or it ran out
                of time. An older result is deleted only when exactly the same check runs again; otherwise it stays
                until you remove the extension or clear its storage.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Exchange rates</th>
              <td>
                Each day’s rates are reused for that UTC day. They stay until you remove the extension or clear its
                storage.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableScroll>
      <p>
        A stored result holds the product details read from the page you are on, each store’s prices (with the seller’s
        and dispatcher’s names when another seller’s offer was quoted) and the exchange rates used. Some of those details can be personal: the <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> text, which
        for a signed-in user can hold a first name, a town and a postcode; the page address, which can carry your
        Amazon search terms; and your destination country.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing the extension stores is sent to the developer or to anyone else.
        To delete it, see <a href="/faq/#delete-data">how to delete your data</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="delivery-location">{sections["delivery-location"]}</h2>
      <Callout tone="caution" title="Other stores’ delivery location may change">
        <p>
          Before it reads a store, the extension may set that store’s delivery location to the country in the{" "}
          <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box of the page you started from, and leaves it that way.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <p>
        A delivered price only compares with yours when every store quotes the same destination, so a store that
        delivers elsewhere is set to your destination country before it is read. When that country is known, a note in
        the popup says so before you start.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>How.</strong> On the first page it reads on a store, the extension reads which country the store
          delivers to. If that is already your destination country, nothing is sent. Otherwise it makes the same two
          requests the store’s own <span class="nowrap">“Choose your location”</span> box makes, in your own browser
          session: it loads the location form, taking only its security token and list of countries, then submits your
          country. From version 0.2.0 the requests go from the extension in the background, and again from the store’s
          own page in a background tab when the store refuses them; before, they always go from the store’s page. Each
          store is set at most once per check.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Only a country.</strong> It never sets a name, town, postcode or address. Your saved addresses, cart
          and orders are not touched, and nothing is ever bought.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Left that way.</strong> The extension never restores the previous location. Your own tabs on that
          store show the new one after their next reload, and later checks find the store already set.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not the store you started from.</strong> Its delivery location is never changed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Country, not address.</strong> When the page you are on shows a town and a postcode, an address in
          that store’s own country, the other stores are set to that country. Their quotes are then for the country,
          not your exact address.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For example, start from a product on amazon.com with <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to Switzerland”</span>.
        The check sets amazon.de, amazon.fr and the other stores to Switzerland, except any store that{" "}
        <a href="#when-a-store-cannot-be-set">cannot be set</a>. A ranked store’s breakdown sheet says “Delivery
        location set to Switzerland” when the check changed it. Afterwards amazon.de shows Switzerland in its{" "}
        <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box until you change it.
      </p>

      <h3 id="changing-it-back">Changing it back</h3>
      <p>
        To change a store back, pick another location in its <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box, at any
        time. Removing the extension does not change a store’s delivery location back.
      </p>

      <h3 id="when-a-store-cannot-be-set">When a store cannot be set to your country</h3>
      <p>A store keeps quoting its previous place when:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>its location form does not offer your country (amazon.com.au offers few countries),</li>
        <li>your destination is the store’s own country, which Amazon sets only by postcode, or</li>
        <li>Amazon refuses the change.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Its price is then for another place, so it is never ranked. It is listed under “N marketplaces could not be
        compared” as “Could not set delivery location”, with a note such as “This store does not offer delivery to
        Switzerland.”
      </p>

      <h2 id="remote-code">{sections["remote-code"]}</h2>
      <p>
        The extension runs no remote code. Every script it runs is bundled in its package. It injects only its own
        packaged files and functions into Amazon pages, and it never fetches code to run. From version 0.2.0, the
        Amazon pages it downloads are read as data in an inert document, and none of their scripts runs.
      </p>

      <p class="closing">
        For everything the extension reads, keeps and sends, see the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>. For short
        answers to common questions, see the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  article :global(.shot) {
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .host-list {
    margin-top: 6px;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .host-list li + li {
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  .closing {
    margin-top: 2.5em;
    padding-top: 1.25em;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
